<!--BlockML Documentation — create or overwrite the A01 DocSetPlan
  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a01_planning.task.A01_01_CreatePlan-->
<task:A01_01_CreatePlan xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:task="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a01_planning.task"
  xmlns:workflow="org.blockml.bml.transformer.workflow"
  xmlns:source="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.lib.source"
  xmlns:lrule="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.lib.rule"
  xmlns:type="org.blockml.bml.type">

  <baseType>
    workflow:InstructionTask
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A task that creates or overwrites BmlDocSetPlan
  </is>
  <documentation>
    A01 planning task: create BmlDocSetPlan as the TOC plan for the
    documentation site. An existing plan is overwritten.

    - **contents** — site-wide goal of the documentation
    - **authorativeSources** — content basis (BlockMLSourceSet)
    - **topLevelGroups** — coarse areas; each becomes the first
      PageGroupPlan under Root (today: Documentation)
    - **maxGroupSize** — maximum entries in any one group, and thus
      the maximum number of pages per level
    - **Pages** — every PagePlan must set scope, readerOutcome,
      learningObjective, exclusion, and supportingPoint
  </documentation>
  <properties>
    <contents type="type:Text">
      <documentation>
        Intended content of the whole documentation site — the overall
        goal that the TOC plan must cover.
      </documentation>
      <value>
      A developer handbook for BlockML Core, written in English.

The documentation should teach the fundamental mental models required to understand BlockML and use it correctly. It should not merely enumerate language features. Important concepts must be explained individually, even when they are technically sub-concepts of another topic.

Each concept listed below should receive a dedicated page. Overview pages should explain how related concepts fit together, while dedicated pages should explain the individual concept in depth.

The handbook should be practical and example-driven. Concept pages should explain what a concept is, why it exists, how it relates to the rest of BlockML, how it is expressed in BML, and how it is used in practice.

The following concepts must be covered on dedicated pages:

* **Everything is a Block**
  Explain the fundamental BlockML mental model: the concepts of a domain are modeled as Blocks.

* **Blocks**
  Explain what a Block is, how Blocks are defined, identified, referenced, and used, and distinguish Block definitions from their concrete use or configuration.

* **Block Definitions and Instances**
  Explain the distinction between defining a reusable concept and creating/configuring an instance of that concept.

* **Inheritance**
  Explain specialization through base types, inherited definitions and members, effective definitions, and the role of abstract and final Blocks.

* **Contracts**
  Explain Contracts as a mechanism for composing reusable definitions and behavior, and distinguish Contracts from inheritance.

* **Namespaces and Fully Qualified Names**
  Explain how Blocks are organized and uniquely identified, including packages, namespaces, FQNs, namespace prefixes, and name resolution.

* **Identifiers and References**
  Explain the distinction between identifiers and validated references, including Type Identifiers, Type References, and abbreviated references through namespaces.

* **Type System**
  Provide the mental model of BlockML's type system and explain how the different kinds of types relate to Blocks, members, and values.

* **Primitive Types**
  Explain primitive value types and their role in the type system.

* **Struct Types**
  Explain structured values, their fields, configuration, and use.

* **Enum Types**
  Explain enumerated value types and enum values.

* **Primitive Entities**
  Explain how primitive values can represent identifiable entities and why this concept exists.

* **Types and Values**
  Explain the fundamental distinction between a type describing possible values and an instance or member holding/configuring an actual value.

* **Generic Types**
  Explain generic type definitions, type parameters, and concrete generic type applications.

* **Members**
  Provide an overview of the Block member model and explain how Properties, Aggregations, Associations, and Capabilities relate to each other.

* **Properties**
  Explain typed values owned by a Block, including their definition, configuration, inheritance, and common usage patterns.

* **Aggregations**
  Explain ownership and containment relationships between Blocks.

* **Associations**
  Explain non-owning references and relationships between Blocks, and contrast them with Aggregations.

* **Capabilities**
  Explain how Blocks describe behavior and callable functionality.

* **Member Inheritance and Overrides**
  Explain how inherited members are combined, refined, overridden, restricted, or finalized.

* **Visibility**
  Explain visibility of Blocks and members and how visibility affects their use from other contexts.

* **Multiplicity and Collections**
  Explain optional, singular, and multiple values and relationships, and how collection semantics are represented throughout BlockML.

* **Constraints**
  Explain declarative restrictions on valid models and values, when constraints are evaluated, and how they differ from ordinary type information.

* **Suggestions**
  Explain non-mandatory modeling guidance and clearly distinguish Suggestions from Constraints.

* **Expressions**
  Explain BlockML expressions, references inside expressions, evaluation context, and how expressions are used by other Core concepts.

* **Value Configuration**
  Explain how values and Block instances are configured in BML, including primitive, structured, collection, enum, reference, and nested values.

* **Composition**
  Explain composition of Blocks and how composition relates to Aggregations, inheritance, and other reuse mechanisms.

* **Sub-Blocks**
  Explain nested or subordinate Block definitions, their identity, scope, and relationship to their containing Block.

* **Embedded Blocks**
  Explain embedded Block definitions or instances and distinguish them from referenced or independently defined Blocks.

* **Documenting Blocks**
  Explain the documentation facilities that are part of Block definitions, including descriptions, examples, and references where applicable.

* **BML**
  Explain BML as the human-authored representation of BlockML models and establish the relationship between the conceptual BlockML model and its XML representation.

* **BML Namespaces**
  Explain XML namespace usage in BML, namespace declarations and prefixes, and distinguish XML namespace mechanics from BlockML's conceptual naming and reference model.

The handbook should prioritize conceptual understanding over implementation details. Compiler internals, BOM implementation classes, renderer-specific behavior, and application-specific Block libraries should only be introduced when they are necessary to understand the public BlockML Core model.

The result should enable a developer who is new to BlockML to build a coherent mental model of the language rather than merely learn its syntax.


      </value>
    </contents>
    <maxGroupSize type="type:Integer">
      <documentation>
        Maximum number of direct entries a PageGroupPlan may have
        (PagePlan and nested PageGroupPlan counted together). Also the
        maximum number of pages per level.
      </documentation>
      <value>30</value>
    </maxGroupSize>
    <instructions type="type:Text" override="true">
      <value>
        <![CDATA[
          Create or overwrite org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a01_planning.data.BmlDocSetPlan
          as the TOC plan for the documentation site. Do not create or edit
          DocSets, Pages, or PageGroups. Do not write outside a01.

          Inputs that produce the plan:

          contents describes the intended content of the whole site:

          ${ #this.contents }

          authorativeSources on this task are the content basis. Domain
          claims about what BlockML Core is and how it is used may come
          only from those sources. Other Blocks may be used as structural
          templates (DocSetPlan / PageGroupPlan / PagePlan member shape
          and nesting), not as domain content.

          topLevelGroups is the coarse split of the site into top-level
          areas. Read this task's topLevelGroups aggregation. Each
          TextEntity.value is one top-level menu area and becomes the
          first PageGroupPlan under Root. Today that is typically
          Documentation. Later this list may grow; later specialized
          Blocks may replace TextEntity with richer area descriptions.
          Use the authored values. Do not invent extra top-level areas.

          maxGroupSize is the maximum number of direct entries any one
          group may have, and therefore the maximum number of pages per
          level: ${ #this.maxGroupSize }.

          Existing BmlDocSetPlan content is never authoritative. If the
          plan already exists, keep only the FQN / file path so the same
          Block is overwritten. Do not treat current titles, groups,
          page names, or planning fields as required. Redesign the TOC from the
          inputs above.

          1. Open the authoritative sources
          Resolve this task's authorativeSources TypeSelectors (scan type
          for each selector on org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.lib.source.BlockMLSourceSet).
          That whitelist is:
          org.blockml.bml.constraint.**, org.blockml.bml.core.**,
          org.blockml.bml.definition.**, org.blockml.bml.documentation.**,
          org.blockml.bml.exception.**, org.blockml.bml.group.**,
          org.blockml.bml.language.**, org.blockml.bml.mapping.**,
          org.blockml.bml.meta.**, org.blockml.bml.relation.**,
          org.blockml.bml.service.**, org.blockml.bml.status.**,
          org.blockml.bml.type.**, org.blockml.bml.unit.**,
          org.blockml.bml.validation.**.

          For each candidate, inspect is, documentation, and references —
          do not guess from the FQN alone. These Blocks decide which
          topics the handbook must cover.

          2. Design the TOC from contents, sources, and topLevelGroups
          contents is the overall site goal. authorativeSources supply
          what is true about BlockML Core. topLevelGroups partition the
          site into coarse areas.

          For each topLevelGroups value, decide how that area should be
          structured as a TOC: which pages the documentation needs, how
          they group, and the learning path. Design a developer handbook
          — how to use BlockML Core, what it is for, examples, and
          how-tos — in English.

          Use the current BmlDocSetPlan only as a structural template:
          - RootPageGroup (title Root) owns one PageGroupPlan per
            top-level area
          - a Documentation area starts with an index / menu page
            (relative name docs.TableOfContents, scope that describes
            the documentation menu, entry points, and navigation
            overview) and then nested section PageGroups
          - section groups use a visible title and a stable *PageGroup
            name (for example GettingStartedPageGroup)
          - each PagePlan has a relative name (for example docs.Intro)
            plus scope, readerOutcome, learningObjective, exclusion,
            and supportingPoint
          - homePage points at the index / menu page when the area has
            one

          Typical section titles in a handbook (Getting Started, Core
          Ideas, Types, Behaviour, Libraries, Authoring, Deeper Topics,
          How-to) are hints, not a required list. Invent, merge, split,
          or drop sections as contents and the sources require.

          3. Apply maxGroupSize
          No PageGroupPlan.plans may contain more than maxGroupSize
          direct children (PagePlan and nested PageGroupPlan counted
          together). That is also the maximum number of pages at one
          level. Split a group when it would exceed
          ${ #this.maxGroupSize }.

          4. Write planning fields on every PagePlan
          Inspect org.blockml.bml.docgen.plan.PagePlan before writing.
          Author each field as an attribute on the PagePlan instance
          (for example scope="…"). Values are type:Text. They are
          planning intent for later sectors — not body text, not a
          purpose sentence, and not finished handbook prose. Ground
          every field in contents and the bound sources. Distinct
          pages must have a distinct primary scope. Cover the site
          goal without dumping the whole handbook onto one page.

          Set the five fields as follows:

          scope (required — ValuePresent)
          What the page covers: the topical content extent. Name the
          topics, concepts, and aspects this page must treat. This is
          the fundamental split of the site goal. Write a concise
          topic list or short extent sentence, not a lecture. Example:
          "Block definition versus Block instance, class and object
          analogy, defining a reusable concept versus configuring an
          instance of it". Two pages must not share the same primary
          scope. Omit this field only if the PagePlan is invalid.

          readerOutcome
          What the reader should take away after reading: what they
          should understand, know, or be able to distinguish. Write
          from the reader's side (capability after the page), not
          from the author's side (what the page contains). Example:
          "The reader can tell a Block definition from a Block
          instance and knows which one they are authoring."

          learningObjective
          What the page intends to teach: the didactic goal — which
          knowledge, understanding, or skill it must convey. Write
          from the page's teaching intent. This is not the same as
          readerOutcome: learningObjective is the lesson; readerOutcome
          is the leftover competence. Example: "Teach the definition
          versus instance distinction so the reader can apply it when
          writing BML."

          exclusion
          What the page deliberately does not cover. Bound scope
          against related topics, especially where another planned
          page owns that topic. Name the neighbouring concepts and
          leave them to those pages. Do not list the whole rest of
          the handbook — only the overlaps that would otherwise
          leak onto this page. Example: "Do not teach inheritance,
          contracts, or member overrides; those belong on their own
          pages."

          supportingPoint
          What supports the page's main message without being the
          central subject. Complementary claims, arguments,
          connections, or facts the later author needs in order to
          make the main topic clear. Do not put a second primary
          topic here — that belongs on another PagePlan. Example:
          "The class/object analogy helps, but the page is about
          BlockML definition versus instance, not OOP."

          Every PagePlan must set all five. scope must be present.
          If a field would only repeat scope, tighten it until each
          field has its own job. Index / menu pages still need all
          five: scope is the menu and entry points; exclusion is
          conceptual body content.

          5. Resolve the plan FQN
          Plan FQN =
          org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a01_planning.data.BmlDocSetPlan
          Write only this a01 plan. Example of the target path:
          packages/bmldocs/blocks/org/blockml/bml/bmldocs/a01_planning/data/BmlDocSetPlan.bml

          6. Write BmlDocSetPlan
          Each plan must:
          - use baseType org.blockml.bml.docgen.plan.DocSetPlan
          - use BmlDocSetPlan as the Block name
          - set is to one short identity sentence from contents
          - set isNot to contrast with a concrete DocSet and with a
            documentation Page
          - set documentation to restate the site goal and to name
            that later sectors materialize Pages and PageGroups
          - override title, summary, and version from contents and
            the designed TOC
          - override homePage to the planned index / menu page when
            one exists (for example page:TableOfContents)
          - fill rootPageGroupPlan as:
            RootPageGroup (title Root) → one PageGroupPlan per
            topLevelGroups entry (title = that TextEntity.value;
            name a stable *PageGroup identifier, for example
            Documentation → DocsPageGroup) → nested PageGroupPlan
            and PagePlan entries
          - each PagePlan: name plus the five Text attributes
            scope, readerOutcome, learningObjective, exclusion,
            supportingPoint (see step 4). Example:
            <dplan:PagePlan name="docs.Blocks"
              scope="…" readerOutcome="…"
              learningObjective="…" exclusion="…"
              supportingPoint="…" />
          - each PageGroupPlan: name + title + plans
          - omit every other aggregation

          7. Export BmlDocSetPlan from the catalog
          Export org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a01_planning.data.BmlDocSetPlan
          from org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.Library if it is not already in
          the catalog. Do not remove catalog entries of other sectors.
        ]]>
      </value>
    </instructions>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <rules override="true">
      <composition>
        <lrule:AppropriatePageGranularityRule />
        <lrule:ProgressivePageOrderRule />
        <lrule:StablePageIdentityRule />
        <lrule:ClearPagePurposeRule />
        <lrule:DistinctPagePurposeRule />
        <lrule:CompleteTopicCoverageRule />
      </composition>
    </rules>
    <topLevelGroups type="type:TextEntity[]">
      <documentation>
        Coarse top-level documentation areas. Each entry becomes the
        first PageGroupPlan under Root (today: Documentation). Later
        specialized Blocks may replace TextEntity with richer area
        descriptions.
      </documentation>
      <composition>
        <type:TextEntity value="Documentation" />
      </composition>
    </topLevelGroups>
    <authorativeSources override="true">
      <composition>
        <source:BlockMLSourceSet />
      </composition>
    </authorativeSources>
  </aggregations>
</task:A01_01_CreatePlan>
